When gypsum is heated at temperature above \( 393 \mathrm{~K} \) than, there no water of crystallization is left and anhydrous calcium<br>sulphate \( \left(\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}\right) \) is formed. This is known as dead burnt plaster.<br>\[
\mathrm{CaSO}_{4} \cdot 2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O} \rightarrow \underset{\text { Gypsum }}{\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}}+2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}
\]
